        "rapidmlx/qwen3.6:35b-a3b",
        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "p-limit's limiter (pLimit in index.js) has clearQueue(), which discards the tasks still waiting to run but leaves their promises unsettled forever, so code that awaits them (with Promise.all, for example) never continues. Add a rejectOnClear option to the options object pLimit accepts, pLimit({concurrency, rejectOnClear}), defaulting to false; today pLimit({concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: true}) silently ignores it. With rejectOnClear: true, clearQueue() must reject the promise of every task that has not started with an error whose name is \"AbortError\" (an Error, like the reason of AbortSignal.abort()), at once rather than after the running tasks finish; running tasks are not cancelled and settle as usual; the function of a discarded task is never called; pendingCount is 0 afterwards; and the limiter keeps working for tasks added later. For example, with const limit = pLimit({concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: true}), adding three tasks that each wait 40 ms and calling limit.clearQueue() after one microtask leaves pendingCount 0, resolves the first task and rejects the other two with AbortError. Without the option, or with rejectOnClear: false, clearQueue() behaves exactly as today. Any rejectOnClear other than true, false or undefined (for example \"nope\", 1 or null) makes pLimit throw a TypeError. limitFunction(function_, options) must accept and validate the same options: limitFunction(fn, {concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: \"nope\"}) throws a TypeError, and limitFunction(fn, {concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: true}) runs fn as usual. Declare the option in the Options type in index.d.ts as an optional boolean, so that pLimit({concurrency: 1, rejectOnClear: true}) type-checks and a non-boolean value is a type error. What must not change: pLimit(number) and pLimit({concurrency}) work as today; concurrency is still validated (pLimit(0) and pLimit({concurrency: 0}) throw a TypeError); no more than the concurrency limit of tasks run at once; tasks resolve with their results and reject with their errors (a synchronous throw included); arguments given as limit(fn, ...arguments) reach fn; and limit.map, activeCount, pendingCount and setting limit.concurrency (raising it starts waiting tasks) behave as today. The source is index.js, with its type declarations in index.d.ts; the tests are test.js (ava) and the type tests index.test-d.ts (tsd). Add regression tests for this behaviour to the project's existing test suite. The project's tests run with npm test (xo, then ava, then tsd).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npm test) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes."
      ],
